1
00:01:03,597 --> 00:01:05,589
You've got a problem? - No.

2
00:01:34,194 --> 00:01:35,787
What do you think?

3
00:01:41,735 --> 00:01:43,203
How come you're
driving the Plymouth?

4
00:01:44,571 --> 00:01:46,039
Started right up.

5
00:01:46,206 --> 00:01:47,970
Runs like a top.

6
00:01:48,141 --> 00:01:49,700
She a beauty?

7
00:01:49,876 --> 00:01:51,674
You haven't had that
car out in ten years.

8
00:01:51,845 --> 00:01:54,508
- I want to show it to Martha.
- Martha who?

9
00:01:54,681 --> 00:01:56,479
Well, that's why I asked
you to meet me here.

10
00:01:57,918 --> 00:01:59,284
Martha Jordan.

11
00:01:59,653 --> 00:02:01,281
Hut, hut, hut. Yeah. BOY 2: Oh.

12
00:02:02,956 --> 00:02:04,151
Haven't seen her in 30 years.

13
00:02:04,324 --> 00:02:05,485
Called out of the blue, heh.

14
00:02:05,659 --> 00:02:07,389
Want you to meet her.

15
00:02:23,944 --> 00:02:26,778
Hey, hey, lady, uh,
here, let me help you.

16
00:02:26,947 --> 00:02:29,781
- Handle gets stuck sometimes.
- Oh, thank you.

17
00:02:29,950 --> 00:02:31,680
Mm. Mm.

18
00:02:35,889 --> 00:02:38,051
Why does her
name sound familiar?

19
00:02:38,225 --> 00:02:40,854
Well, it was a long time
ago. You don't remember.

20
00:02:41,028 --> 00:02:45,295
Her husband was my
first client, Cyrus Jordan.

21
00:02:46,933 --> 00:02:51,303
<i>Worked in the diner my dad always
ate at back home in Mount Harlan.</i>

22
00:02:51,471 --> 00:02:53,133
<i>It was in the early '60s.</i>

23
00:02:53,306 --> 00:02:55,775
<i>Things were real different then.</i>

24
00:03:04,785 --> 00:03:06,344
Cyrus. CYRUS: Yes, sir?

25
00:03:06,520 --> 00:03:08,989
Deputy needs to
have his order done.

26
00:03:11,658 --> 00:03:13,024
What can I get
y'all this morning?

27
00:03:13,193 --> 00:03:16,186
Whip me up some of them biscuits
and gravy like the sheriff always eats.

28
00:03:16,363 --> 00:03:18,457
- Yes, sir.
- Fried eggs and grits for me.

29
00:03:18,632 --> 00:03:20,362
Alrighty.

30
00:03:22,402 --> 00:03:24,132
Thank you.

31
00:03:26,973 --> 00:03:29,067
Pete. PETE: Charlie.

32
00:03:30,644 --> 00:03:32,237
- Cyrus.
- Mr. Matlock,

33
00:03:32,412 --> 00:03:34,412
I'll have your eggs ready
for you in just a minute.

34
00:03:34,581 --> 00:03:36,015
All right.

35
00:03:36,383 --> 00:03:38,545
I hear you've been
blueberry-picking.

36
00:03:39,920 --> 00:03:42,321
Yes, sir. We got
two pies back there.

37
00:03:42,489 --> 00:03:44,856
Ain't too sure how long
they're gonna last, though.

38
00:03:45,025 --> 00:03:48,860
You want me to save you a piece?

39
00:03:53,300 --> 00:03:55,667
Biscuits and gravy,
Cyrus. Make it fast.

40
00:03:56,169 --> 00:03:57,398
Coming right up, sheriff.

41
00:03:57,571 --> 00:04:00,507
Hope you weren't planning on
a leisurely breakfast, sergeant.

42
00:04:00,674 --> 00:04:03,114
When we get done here, we
gotta get out to Willie Dodd's place

43
00:04:03,243 --> 00:04:05,109
and bust up that still
of his once and for all.

44
00:04:05,278 --> 00:04:06,302
Why?

45
00:04:06,480 --> 00:04:10,417
Attorney General's office. Been
raising Cain with me since 8 this morning.

46
00:04:10,584 --> 00:04:13,179
I know what it is,
though. It's the feds.

47
00:04:13,353 --> 00:04:16,221
They just itching for an excuse
to get people like me fired,

48
00:04:16,389 --> 00:04:19,382
so they can bring them
integrationists down here and take over.

49
00:04:19,559 --> 00:04:22,222
That's just what we need is a
bunch of Yankees coming in here

50
00:04:22,395 --> 00:04:23,954
and telling us how to do things.

51
00:04:24,131 --> 00:04:26,657
You'd better get used
to it. Changing times.

52
00:04:27,134 --> 00:04:29,365
That's what they
call it up in Atlanta.

53
00:04:29,536 --> 00:04:33,371
Flushing the South down
the toilet. That's what I call it.

54
00:04:33,540 --> 00:04:34,974
I need some coffee here, boy.

55
00:04:37,310 --> 00:04:39,176
Here. Here.

56
00:04:42,415 --> 00:04:46,750
Yup, I stopped by the dime
store this morning to get a paper.

57
00:04:46,920 --> 00:04:54,123
Frosty told me that Owen Lashley's
pawn shop was robbed last night.

58
00:04:54,394 --> 00:04:56,158
Got any idea who it was?

59
00:04:56,329 --> 00:04:58,423
Like I was just saying, Charlie,

60
00:04:58,598 --> 00:05:01,067
I got more important
matters to attend to right now.

61
00:05:01,234 --> 00:05:03,169
Now, Owen, he'll
just have to wait.

62
00:05:03,336 --> 00:05:06,932
He shouldn't have had all
that cash in there to start with.

63
00:05:11,278 --> 00:05:12,712
I need me another fork, Cyrus.

64
00:05:12,879 --> 00:05:14,939
Someone back there
forgot to wash this one.

65
00:05:15,115 --> 00:05:16,675
All right. I'll get
you one right away.

66
00:05:16,817 --> 00:05:19,048
I'm eating biscuits and
gravy this morning too.

67
00:05:19,219 --> 00:05:20,243
Sure is good.

68
00:05:21,555 --> 00:05:23,456
Nice and clean.

69
00:05:24,124 --> 00:05:25,592
Thing is burned.

70
00:05:25,759 --> 00:05:27,921
The biscuits are hard as rocks.

71
00:05:28,261 --> 00:05:30,662
This is garbage, boy.

72
00:05:30,997 --> 00:05:32,021
Refuse.

73
00:05:33,433 --> 00:05:34,696
It's bad news.

74
00:05:34,868 --> 00:05:36,734
You expect me to pay for it.

75
00:05:36,903 --> 00:05:39,463
But did you really
think I was gonna eat it?

76
00:05:39,639 --> 00:05:42,541
I mean, just how
ignorant do you think I am?

77
00:05:44,778 --> 00:05:47,475
Cyrus, why don't you get
the sheriff some hotcakes?

78
00:05:48,782 --> 00:05:50,580
I don't want no hotcakes.

79
00:05:51,117 --> 00:05:53,018
I want an answer.

80
00:05:53,320 --> 00:05:55,255
You think I'm ignorant, boy.

81
00:05:59,492 --> 00:06:00,687
No, sir.

82
00:06:02,829 --> 00:06:05,321
Then why did you serve me trash?

83
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:09,128
Deputy Hayes there don't
seem to mind the flavor none.

84
00:06:09,302 --> 00:06:11,271
I say it's trash, boy.

85
00:06:12,138 --> 00:06:14,334
You know what I do with trash?

86
00:06:17,043 --> 00:06:19,512
I make trash clean it up.

87
00:06:32,259 --> 00:06:35,593
It does taste
burned, like he said.

88
00:06:36,396 --> 00:06:38,991
Maybe if you took a couple
of meals out of his wages,

89
00:06:39,165 --> 00:06:41,862
he'd be more careful next time.

90
00:06:44,704 --> 00:06:45,797
Clean it up.

91
00:06:46,706 --> 00:06:47,867
Hurry.

92
00:07:22,008 --> 00:07:23,772
You want a ride?

93
00:07:23,944 --> 00:07:27,244
Heh, no, thank you, Mr. Matlock.
Uh, I can walk just fine.

94
00:07:27,414 --> 00:07:28,507
Oh, come on. Get in.

95
00:07:28,682 --> 00:07:32,312
You don't wanna wear a hole in
them new shoes before you have to.

96
00:07:32,485 --> 00:07:34,818
Come on.

97
00:07:35,255 --> 00:07:37,986
- All right. Thank you.
- Yeah.

98
00:07:38,792 --> 00:07:40,488
My wife bought me these shoes.

99
00:07:40,660 --> 00:07:43,323
Oh, they're nice. They
look like they cost a lot.

100
00:07:43,496 --> 00:07:45,296
She didn't tell me how
much she paid for them.

101
00:07:45,432 --> 00:07:47,992
She just made me promise,
uh, to take real good care of them.

102
00:07:48,168 --> 00:07:49,966
Oh, yeah, heh.

103
00:07:52,138 --> 00:07:55,905
You know, the sheriff
was having a bad day

104
00:07:56,076 --> 00:07:59,308
way before he got to
the diner this morning.

105
00:07:59,479 --> 00:08:02,711
It wasn't you or your
cooking that made him mad.

106
00:08:02,882 --> 00:08:06,512
Yeah. Well, I don't pay
much attention to stuff like that.

107
00:08:06,686 --> 00:08:09,918
Mr. Matlock, I just let
it roll right off my back.

108
00:08:10,090 --> 00:08:12,685
Hmm, do you?

109
00:08:12,859 --> 00:08:16,057
Mm-hm, sort of
like water off a duck.

110
00:08:17,097 --> 00:08:20,795
You're a better man than I am,
Cyrus Jordan, I'll tell you that.

111
00:08:27,841 --> 00:08:28,865
Are you all right?

112
00:08:30,243 --> 00:08:31,973
Yeah. I'm all right.

113
00:08:32,145 --> 00:08:33,909
This is Mr. Charlie Matlock.

114
00:08:34,080 --> 00:08:37,482
- Is it true what they said?
- I don't know, son. What did they say?

115
00:08:37,650 --> 00:08:39,290
That the sheriff dumped
food all over you.

116
00:08:41,521 --> 00:08:43,114
Heh, yeah. That's all right.

117
00:08:43,289 --> 00:08:45,087
Why didn't you hit him?

118
00:08:45,258 --> 00:08:46,978
I don't want you talking
like that. Hear me?

119
00:08:47,127 --> 00:08:49,647
Sheriff or no sheriff, I would
have shoved his face in the gravy

120
00:08:49,796 --> 00:08:51,662
and drowned him in it.

121
00:08:51,831 --> 00:08:55,893
Now, you quit that. And if you don't get
your butt back in the house, you better.

122
00:08:56,069 --> 00:08:58,163
Eye for an eye,
tooth for a tooth.

123
00:08:58,338 --> 00:09:00,569
Says so right in the Bible.

124
00:09:01,374 --> 00:09:03,070
Hush up.

125
00:09:07,013 --> 00:09:08,743
Well...

126
00:09:09,816 --> 00:09:11,842
Your boy's growing up.

127
00:09:12,018 --> 00:09:13,782
Quoting the Bible
and everything.

128
00:09:13,953 --> 00:09:16,013
- Yeah. When it suits him.
- Oh.

129
00:09:17,057 --> 00:09:20,323
Yes, that's the way
it is with most of us.

130
00:09:20,527 --> 00:09:23,861
Where did you get
that old '46 Plymouth?

131
00:09:24,197 --> 00:09:25,825
Mr. Harriman give it to me

132
00:09:25,999 --> 00:09:28,764
for painting his house and
fixing up his yard last year.

133
00:09:28,935 --> 00:09:32,895
She ran real good for a day
or two, then she just quit on me.

134
00:09:33,706 --> 00:09:37,734
How about this, next time you
make a batch of blueberry pies,

135
00:09:37,911 --> 00:09:38,935
make me one.

136
00:09:39,112 --> 00:09:41,604
When I come out here
to get it, I'll bring the tools

137
00:09:41,781 --> 00:09:44,148
and see what I can
do. How about it?

138
00:09:44,717 --> 00:09:47,949
- Heh, that would be fine.
- Heh, see you.

139
00:09:49,456 --> 00:09:52,449
Does the prosecution wish to
cross-examine the defendant?

140
00:09:56,396 --> 00:09:57,557
Mr. Matlock.

141
00:09:59,732 --> 00:10:02,998
Yes, Your Honor. Thank you.

142
00:10:06,473 --> 00:10:09,773
So you've never been
at Jim Desmond's barn?

143
00:10:10,076 --> 00:10:12,045
I've never set foot in his barn.

144
00:10:12,212 --> 00:10:14,012
When's the last time
you were on his property?

145
00:10:14,180 --> 00:10:16,513
Never set foot on
his property, neither.

146
00:10:16,683 --> 00:10:19,551
- Never?
- Nope. Never.

147
00:10:22,188 --> 00:10:26,956
When you gave blood last year,
they told you your blood type.

148
00:10:27,327 --> 00:10:29,796
Happen to remember what it is?

149
00:10:31,030 --> 00:10:32,692
AB something, heh, or other.

150
00:10:32,866 --> 00:10:34,232
AB negative.

151
00:10:35,602 --> 00:10:36,661
Pretty rare.

152
00:10:36,836 --> 00:10:39,931
But wouldn't you know,

153
00:10:40,106 --> 00:10:44,168
it's the same type that was
found on this strand of barbed wire,

154
00:10:44,344 --> 00:10:47,007
which, as we just heard
from Deputy Gadell,

155
00:10:47,180 --> 00:10:50,014
the sheriff removed from
the fence besides Jim's barn

156
00:10:50,183 --> 00:10:52,118
the morning after the theft.

157
00:10:52,285 --> 00:10:55,483
Now, they believe

158
00:10:56,055 --> 00:10:59,856
whoever broke into the
barn, stole those saddles,

159
00:11:00,026 --> 00:11:02,188
threw them over the fence,

160
00:11:02,362 --> 00:11:05,355
then crawled between the
wires to make his getaway,

161
00:11:05,532 --> 00:11:06,693
scratching himself

162
00:11:06,866 --> 00:11:11,566
and leaving AB negative blood
on these barbs in the process.

163
00:11:11,738 --> 00:11:14,867
Well, AB negative or
XYZ positive, who cares?

164
00:11:15,041 --> 00:11:16,703
Finding it there
don't make it mine.

165
00:11:16,876 --> 00:11:21,007
Very true. But it
got me to thinking.

166
00:11:21,181 --> 00:11:25,016
If it had been me getting all cut
up on rusty barbed wire like that,

167
00:11:26,219 --> 00:11:30,122
first thing I would have
done is hightail it to a doctor.

168
00:11:30,990 --> 00:11:33,391
Ever been to see Doc Wilshire?

169
00:11:33,560 --> 00:11:34,687
Of course I have.

170
00:11:34,861 --> 00:11:37,763
Everybody in Mount Harlan
has. He's the only doctor we got.

171
00:11:37,931 --> 00:11:39,797
When was the last time
you saw him, Darryl?

172
00:11:41,301 --> 00:11:42,394
I don't remember.

173
00:11:42,569 --> 00:11:45,437
July 12th, 10 a.m.,

174
00:11:45,605 --> 00:11:48,939
the morning after somebody
broke into Jim Desmond's barn.

175
00:11:49,642 --> 00:11:52,237
Doc Wilshire cleaned
the cuts on your back,

176
00:11:52,412 --> 00:11:53,903
gave you a tetanus shot

177
00:11:54,080 --> 00:11:57,380
and sent you on
your way. Isn't that so?

178
00:12:03,156 --> 00:12:05,091
No further questions.

179
00:12:11,598 --> 00:12:13,863
That was good, son.

180
00:12:14,434 --> 00:12:17,268
Kind of let him
convict himself, heh.

181
00:12:17,437 --> 00:12:18,666
I tried to.

182
00:12:18,838 --> 00:12:20,329
Yeah. That was good.

183
00:12:20,506 --> 00:12:23,874
Set the trap and let
him walk right into it.

184
00:12:25,979 --> 00:12:29,416
Dad, there's something
about being a DA

185
00:12:29,582 --> 00:12:32,552
that just doesn't
sit right with me.

186
00:12:32,719 --> 00:12:35,621
I don't like sending
people to jail.

187
00:12:36,155 --> 00:12:37,817
Well, it's steady work.

188
00:12:37,991 --> 00:12:40,392
When I was your age,
that's all there was to it.

189
00:12:40,994 --> 00:12:43,463
Yeah, but I'd rather
do something else.

190
00:12:43,630 --> 00:12:45,064
What?

191
00:12:45,665 --> 00:12:49,363
Well, been thinking
about this for a while.

192
00:12:49,802 --> 00:12:52,203
I'm quitting the DA's office.

193
00:12:52,372 --> 00:12:54,034
Practice law on my
own from now on,

194
00:12:54,207 --> 00:12:56,802
start defending people,
instead of prosecuting them.

195
00:12:57,410 --> 00:12:59,709
Oh. Uh-huh.

196
00:13:00,046 --> 00:13:03,813
Kind of like Elmo
Erickson, the drunk.

197
00:13:05,251 --> 00:13:07,331
Dad, just because the only
criminal attorney you know

198
00:13:07,487 --> 00:13:09,251
is an alcoholic doesn't
mean they all are.

199
00:13:09,422 --> 00:13:11,084
No. No. I know.

200
00:13:11,257 --> 00:13:13,954
Carlton Reeves,
he ain't no drunk.

201
00:13:15,194 --> 00:13:17,925
Of course, he never
got married neither, heh.

202
00:13:19,432 --> 00:13:22,334
I'm going to the office.
I'll talk to you later.

203
00:13:26,606 --> 00:13:29,007
You checked all the
windows and doors?

204
00:13:29,175 --> 00:13:32,202
You didn't find nothing
open or busted?

205
00:13:32,378 --> 00:13:33,676
No. I come in this morning.

206
00:13:33,846 --> 00:13:35,576
Everything was
clean as a whistle.

207
00:13:35,748 --> 00:13:38,946
I went to open the cash register
and I realized I'd been robbed.

208
00:13:39,118 --> 00:13:41,246
And nothing else in
the shop was missing?

209
00:13:41,421 --> 00:13:43,083
No cameras? No jewelry?

210
00:13:43,256 --> 00:13:46,920
No. Just the money. $693.

211
00:13:47,093 --> 00:13:50,552
Owen, how many
times we got to tell you?

212
00:13:50,730 --> 00:13:53,222
Keep your money in
the bank, not at the store.

213
00:13:53,399 --> 00:13:54,958
It's a pawnshop, Wes.

214
00:13:55,134 --> 00:13:56,466
I gotta have money.

215
00:13:56,869 --> 00:13:58,462
And here's the worst part.

216
00:13:58,638 --> 00:13:59,970
My momma's gone to Florida,

217
00:14:00,573 --> 00:14:03,008
and I wrote her phone
number on a 50-dollar bill.

218
00:14:03,176 --> 00:14:04,542
Now I can't call her.

219
00:14:04,711 --> 00:14:09,206
Are you saying a stolen bill's got a
phone number written down on it?

220
00:14:09,382 --> 00:14:13,478
- Yes, sir.
- On the front or the back?

221
00:14:13,653 --> 00:14:17,181
Well, it was on the front, right
next door to Ulysses S. Grant.

222
00:14:17,357 --> 00:14:18,985
Now, that's what I call a lead.

223
00:14:19,158 --> 00:14:21,127
I'll get on it first
thing in the morning.

224
00:14:21,294 --> 00:14:23,320
No. You won't.

225
00:14:23,496 --> 00:14:27,661
Not till the Attorney General's
office quits breathing down my neck.

226
00:14:27,834 --> 00:14:29,325
I'm gonna personally oversee

227
00:14:29,502 --> 00:14:31,903
each and every criminal
investigation we undertake.

228
00:14:32,071 --> 00:14:33,471
I tell you...

229
00:14:33,906 --> 00:14:37,343
Yeah, why don't you head on
home, sonny. I can finish up here.

230
00:14:37,510 --> 00:14:38,842
Sounds good to me.

231
00:14:39,011 --> 00:14:41,446
My old lady just hates it
when I'm late for supper.

232
00:14:41,614 --> 00:14:44,345
- Yeah, get out of here. Yeah.
- See you in the morning.

233
00:14:45,084 --> 00:14:50,648
So this, uh, phone
number in Florida...

234
00:14:51,157 --> 00:14:53,490
Owen, that about
takes care of that.

235
00:14:53,659 --> 00:14:57,721
If and when we find that 50-dollar
bill we may have ourselves a thief.

236
00:14:57,897 --> 00:15:01,026
Uh-oh, somebody
lost their wallet.

237
00:15:01,200 --> 00:15:02,532
Yeah.

238
00:15:02,702 --> 00:15:04,568
Heh, Sully's.

239
00:15:04,737 --> 00:15:08,936
I swear. That man would lose
his head if it weren't bolted on.

240
00:15:09,609 --> 00:15:12,602
Hey, Sully. This here's
the sheriff. Do you read me?

241
00:15:12,779 --> 00:15:15,139
<i>I hear you, Wes.
What's going on?</i>

242
00:15:16,416 --> 00:15:18,078
You left your wallet here, boy.

243
00:15:18,251 --> 00:15:20,532
And where the heck are you,
anyway? I can hardly hear you.

244
00:15:21,354 --> 00:15:23,346
<i>Driving about two
blocks from home.</i>

245
00:15:23,523 --> 00:15:24,991
<i>Just stick it in my desk, okay?</i>

246
00:15:25,158 --> 00:15:27,150
<i>I'll get it in the morning.</i>

247
00:15:29,095 --> 00:15:33,260
Well, you know you're out there
driving around without a license now.

248
00:15:33,433 --> 00:15:35,459
If I see you, I'm gonna
have to arrest you, heh.

249
00:16:43,135 --> 00:16:44,159
Don't you work too hard.

250
00:16:45,538 --> 00:16:48,940
Heh, yeah. Cora,
come here, girl.

251
00:16:49,709 --> 00:16:51,869
You be good in school and
learn something for me today.

252
00:16:52,011 --> 00:16:54,242
All right? Give me some sugar.

253
00:16:56,949 --> 00:17:01,080
Gamble, Mr. Know-It-All, boy,
you gotta work on that temper.

254
00:17:04,957 --> 00:17:05,981
Y'all stand back.

255
00:17:07,260 --> 00:17:08,728
You're under arrest, boy.

256
00:17:08,895 --> 00:17:10,693
- For what?
- Leave my daddy alone.

257
00:17:10,863 --> 00:17:13,264
For murdering Sheriff
Slattery. What the hell you think?

258
00:17:18,204 --> 00:17:19,604
Come on.

259
00:17:34,320 --> 00:17:37,119
My, my, don't you look fine.
- Heh.

260
00:17:37,290 --> 00:17:38,314
Oh, well, heh.

261
00:17:38,491 --> 00:17:39,891
And this is your daughter.

262
00:17:40,059 --> 00:17:42,824
Leanne. I am so pleased
to meet you, Ms. Jordan.

263
00:17:42,995 --> 00:17:46,159
I was just telling Leanne
about you and Cyrus

264
00:17:46,332 --> 00:17:49,097
and that time he was
accused of murder.

265
00:17:49,268 --> 00:17:52,033
I wouldn't be here today if it
wasn't for your granddaddy.

266
00:17:53,406 --> 00:17:54,430
Sit down, honey.

267
00:17:54,607 --> 00:17:57,167
Your daddy never was one
for making a long story short.

268
00:17:58,544 --> 00:17:59,568
So where were you?

269
00:17:59,745 --> 00:18:02,909
Well, I was telling her about
the morning Cyrus was arrested.

270
00:18:03,082 --> 00:18:04,516
Hmm.

271
00:18:06,385 --> 00:18:09,048
<i>Dad called and asked
me to come over for lunch.</i>

272
00:18:18,497 --> 00:18:20,728
You're making me hot dogs.

273
00:18:20,900 --> 00:18:23,961
- They're your favorite, ain't they?
- Yeah.

274
00:18:24,136 --> 00:18:27,163
You've been telling me I've been
eating too many of them for years.

275
00:18:27,340 --> 00:18:28,672
What are you up to?

276
00:18:28,841 --> 00:18:31,003
Just trying to fix you
something you like.

277
00:18:31,177 --> 00:18:32,839
Why?

278
00:18:33,012 --> 00:18:36,312
Oh, I just want you to know that
if you wanna quit your job uptown,

279
00:18:36,482 --> 00:18:38,314
it's all right with me.

280
00:18:38,484 --> 00:18:40,316
And what else?

281
00:18:40,486 --> 00:18:42,421
Well, I...

282
00:18:43,055 --> 00:18:45,456
I might have a client for you.

283
00:18:45,992 --> 00:18:48,621
Hey, Pop, that's great.

284
00:18:49,195 --> 00:18:55,499
Of course, if you take him
on, it'd have to be for free.

285
00:19:03,843 --> 00:19:05,471
I was at home all night.

286
00:19:05,645 --> 00:19:08,547
You can ask
Martha. She'll tell you.

287
00:19:09,749 --> 00:19:11,718
That ain't good enough, is it?

288
00:19:11,884 --> 00:19:13,284
For me, it is.

289
00:19:13,452 --> 00:19:17,219
But not for a jury.

290
00:19:17,390 --> 00:19:20,189
They'll consider
her a little biased.

291
00:19:20,826 --> 00:19:23,819
Any idea how your knife wound
up being used as a murder weapon?

292
00:19:23,996 --> 00:19:25,362
Will you quit saying that?

293
00:19:25,531 --> 00:19:27,329
It ain't my knife!

294
00:19:31,437 --> 00:19:33,668
I use it when I'm working.

295
00:19:35,041 --> 00:19:37,704
Pete says, uh, you
were using that knife

296
00:19:37,877 --> 00:19:40,642
to cut up blueberry
pies yesterday.

297
00:19:42,248 --> 00:19:46,982
Traces of blueberry juice the
lab found on the sheriff's shirt

298
00:19:47,153 --> 00:19:50,146
in one of the stab
wounds confirm that.

299
00:19:50,956 --> 00:19:53,391
I left the knife for Moley to
have cleaned the next morning.

300
00:19:53,559 --> 00:19:55,460
Like always.

301
00:19:56,395 --> 00:20:00,298
Somebody just must have
broke in there and took it

302
00:20:00,466 --> 00:20:01,934
when everybody went home.

303
00:20:03,569 --> 00:20:07,267
Everybody knew about that
run-in you had with the sheriff.

304
00:20:08,107 --> 00:20:10,303
You know how fast word
travels around this town.

305
00:20:10,476 --> 00:20:12,104
It could have been anybody.

306
00:20:14,080 --> 00:20:16,345
They won't let me see Martha.

307
00:20:18,818 --> 00:20:21,344
It wouldn't be so bad in here.

308
00:20:21,587 --> 00:20:24,079
But they just won't
let me see Martha.

309
00:20:28,894 --> 00:20:30,829
Maybe I can get you out on bail.

310
00:20:39,138 --> 00:20:40,436
You can get me out of here?

311
00:20:41,373 --> 00:20:42,932
Maybe.

312
00:20:44,543 --> 00:20:46,136
I used to work with these guys.

313
00:20:46,312 --> 00:20:49,339
Maybe they'll do me a favor.

314
00:20:49,849 --> 00:20:52,944
Look, uh, I can't
make bail money.

315
00:20:53,119 --> 00:20:55,520
So what's the difference?

316
00:20:55,688 --> 00:21:00,389
Well, uh... Ben and
me can help you out.

317
00:21:00,559 --> 00:21:02,585
Uh, Dad.

318
00:21:03,262 --> 00:21:05,322
That's a lot of money.

319
00:21:06,365 --> 00:21:07,890
Uh...

320
00:21:08,567 --> 00:21:10,593
I stand on my own.

321
00:21:10,770 --> 00:21:12,500
Always.

322
00:21:12,772 --> 00:21:14,263
And, uh...

323
00:21:15,775 --> 00:21:18,142
Since I ain't got no money,

324
00:21:19,278 --> 00:21:21,747
I'd just as soon stay in here.

325
00:21:32,024 --> 00:21:33,549
Uh...

326
00:21:34,527 --> 00:21:36,325
Uh, Cyrus.

327
00:21:37,329 --> 00:21:43,929
If I was to buy something from you,
now that wouldn't be charity, would it?

328
00:21:45,204 --> 00:21:47,105
I used to hate that old car.

329
00:21:47,273 --> 00:21:50,641
It would never let me forget
how Mr. Harriman cheated you.

330
00:21:50,810 --> 00:21:53,712
The man knew that car
was gonna break down.

331
00:21:53,879 --> 00:21:56,872
Heh, but I don't
hate it no more.

332
00:21:57,049 --> 00:21:59,075
When is Mr. Matlock
picking it up?

333
00:21:59,251 --> 00:22:01,277
Tomorrow afternoon.

334
00:22:01,754 --> 00:22:03,916
Feels so good to have
your arms around me again.

335
00:22:08,427 --> 00:22:10,419
- There's more.
- Get down!

336
00:22:20,039 --> 00:22:22,565
Get them right now!

337
00:22:31,083 --> 00:22:32,107
Phew.

338
00:22:33,252 --> 00:22:35,050
That's Martha's brother, Brundy.

339
00:22:35,487 --> 00:22:39,390
Her and the young ones are gonna
stay there until this thing is over.

340
00:22:41,227 --> 00:22:42,855
What's wrong with
people, anyway?

341
00:22:43,028 --> 00:22:44,394
I don't know.

342
00:22:48,067 --> 00:22:49,899
You need some help?

343
00:22:50,469 --> 00:22:52,370
No. I think we got
her so she don't jump.

344
00:22:52,538 --> 00:22:54,029
All right.

345
00:22:54,373 --> 00:22:55,898
You ain't gonna
stay here, are you?

346
00:22:56,075 --> 00:22:58,101
Of course I am. This is my home.

347
00:22:59,545 --> 00:23:02,743
Well, I got a feeling them
jackasses might come back.

348
00:23:02,915 --> 00:23:04,008
You can't stay here.

349
00:23:04,917 --> 00:23:07,182
I don't have a choice. I don't
have nowhere else to go.

350
00:23:07,353 --> 00:23:08,377
That ain't so.

351
00:23:08,554 --> 00:23:10,318
I got an extra bed at my house.

352
00:23:11,090 --> 00:23:15,585
Heh, look, no, no, I appreciate
it, but I'll just stay home.

353
00:23:15,761 --> 00:23:17,457
But you can't stay here.

354
00:23:17,630 --> 00:23:19,223
They might do something to you.

355
00:23:19,398 --> 00:23:21,162
Look, I'm gonna
get hung, anyway!

356
00:23:21,333 --> 00:23:23,893
So, what the hell
difference does it make?

357
00:23:24,637 --> 00:23:26,936
He ain't gonna get me
off and we both know it.

358
00:23:29,975 --> 00:23:34,611
Ahem, well,
actually, I think I can.

359
00:23:35,114 --> 00:23:37,948
Or, at least, I intend
to try my damndest.

360
00:23:39,151 --> 00:23:41,985
Well, thinking and trying is a
long way up the road from doing.

361
00:23:42,554 --> 00:23:45,319
I got you out on bail, didn't I?

362
00:23:46,225 --> 00:23:50,356
All the gun-toting jackasses in the
world can't change the facts, Cyrus.

363
00:23:50,529 --> 00:23:52,430
The fact is, you're innocent.

364
00:23:52,598 --> 00:23:53,964
That means something.

365
00:23:55,234 --> 00:23:56,998
Yeah, something to that.

366
00:23:57,169 --> 00:23:58,228
That's right.

367
00:23:58,404 --> 00:24:00,373
Get your stuff
together. We'll wait.

368
00:24:15,921 --> 00:24:18,841
Sorry I can't help with the dishes. I
got a lot of reading to catch up on.

369
00:24:18,991 --> 00:24:21,358
Oh, you done
enough. You go ahead.

370
00:24:21,527 --> 00:24:24,964
Mm, Mr. Matlock, where
y'all learn to cook like this?

371
00:24:25,130 --> 00:24:27,361
Well, let's just say it
wasn't from my father.

372
00:24:28,734 --> 00:24:29,854
I'll see you in the morning.

373
00:24:30,002 --> 00:24:31,868
- Uh, night, heh.
- Night.

374
00:24:33,038 --> 00:24:36,907
- Here, let me help you.
- No, no, no, you just sit there.

375
00:24:37,076 --> 00:24:38,100
Yeah.

376
00:24:39,511 --> 00:24:42,379
- That's a fine house you got here.
- Yeah.

377
00:24:42,548 --> 00:24:44,710
A real fine boy too. You
must be real proud of him.

378
00:24:44,883 --> 00:24:48,149
Well, yeah, no more than
you are of Gamble, though.

379
00:24:48,320 --> 00:24:50,516
He's a fine boy, smart,
got a head on his shoulders.

380
00:24:50,689 --> 00:24:51,748
Yeah.

381
00:24:51,924 --> 00:24:55,019
He got a temper too. Did he
get that from you or Martha?

382
00:24:55,194 --> 00:24:57,095
Um, me, I guess, heh.

383
00:24:59,198 --> 00:25:01,292
He didn't learn to
hold his in yet, huh?

384
00:25:01,467 --> 00:25:04,403
No. He ain't had too
much practice at it.

385
00:25:05,137 --> 00:25:08,301
- Come on, now, let me take this...
- No, no, sit there.

386
00:25:08,474 --> 00:25:11,069
Let's talk about things.

387
00:25:11,243 --> 00:25:14,509
You know, the only place we know
one another is down at the diner.

388
00:25:14,680 --> 00:25:18,310
The way I see it, that's like
not knowing one another at all.

389
00:25:19,351 --> 00:25:22,685
Well, there ain't a whole
lot to know, Mr. Matlock.

390
00:25:23,355 --> 00:25:26,621
Charlie. It's Charlie.

391
00:25:26,792 --> 00:25:28,226
You like cooking for folks?

392
00:25:31,797 --> 00:25:33,425
I like feeding my family.

393
00:25:34,800 --> 00:25:37,599
Uh, look, I ain't too
good at this talking.

394
00:25:37,770 --> 00:25:39,932
So unless you want me to
go in and get on them dishes,

395
00:25:40,105 --> 00:25:42,097
I'm just gonna turn in.

396
00:25:42,274 --> 00:25:45,210
And again, I can't thank
you enough, Mr. Matlock.

397
00:25:45,377 --> 00:25:47,005
Charlie.

398
00:25:47,746 --> 00:25:49,977
No, sir. No, it ain't.

399
00:25:50,149 --> 00:25:52,948
It's Mr. Matlock.

400
00:25:53,318 --> 00:25:54,838
White folks hear me
calling you Charlie

401
00:25:54,987 --> 00:25:57,456
around that diner or
around town, they...

402
00:25:59,992 --> 00:26:02,086
They just don't like that.

403
00:26:04,863 --> 00:26:06,092
Mr. Matlock...

404
00:26:07,966 --> 00:26:09,628
You're a good man,

405
00:26:09,802 --> 00:26:12,499
and I don't mean
you no disrespect,

406
00:26:13,305 --> 00:26:15,331
but some things...

407
00:26:16,208 --> 00:26:17,699
are the way they are.

408
00:26:40,933 --> 00:26:43,402
They burned a cross
on Grandpa's lawn?

409
00:26:43,569 --> 00:26:46,835
When Cyrus sent us
to live with my brother,

410
00:26:47,005 --> 00:26:52,467
it was because he didn't want
us to be there if things turned ugly.

411
00:26:52,644 --> 00:26:55,478
And back then, when a
black man was accused

412
00:26:55,647 --> 00:26:57,673
of murdering a white man,

413
00:26:57,850 --> 00:27:00,649
things could turn real ugly.

414
00:27:01,653 --> 00:27:03,622
- And what happened?
- Hmm.

415
00:27:03,789 --> 00:27:06,109
My father put the fire out
and called the sheriff's office.

416
00:27:08,660 --> 00:27:11,323
<i>Nobody showed up
till the next morning.</i>

417
00:27:11,497 --> 00:27:14,194
<i>Might as well not
shown up at all.</i>

418
00:27:17,336 --> 00:27:19,771
I'm telling you,
it's Mitchell Crane.

419
00:27:19,938 --> 00:27:22,669
He was wearing that ball cap.
You know, where the bill's turned up,

420
00:27:22,841 --> 00:27:25,675
where he looks like
some kind of giant duck.

421
00:27:25,844 --> 00:27:28,245
It was nighttime, Charlie. You
can't be sure what you saw.

422
00:27:28,413 --> 00:27:30,644
That fire's going as
bright as day out here.

423
00:27:31,416 --> 00:27:34,511
Even if it was Mitchell Crane, I
wouldn't have arrested him, anyway.

424
00:27:34,686 --> 00:27:35,847
I mean, what's the crime?

425
00:27:36,321 --> 00:27:38,415
Trespassing.
Destruction of property.

426
00:27:38,891 --> 00:27:40,689
Some of your grass got burned.

427
00:27:40,859 --> 00:27:41,918
It'll grow back.

428
00:27:42,094 --> 00:27:44,086
In fact, them ashes will
probably do it some good.

429
00:27:45,030 --> 00:27:47,431
You're telling me it's all right
to go around burning stuff

430
00:27:47,599 --> 00:27:48,965
in other people's yards?

431
00:27:49,535 --> 00:27:52,972
Look, we'll do everything we can
to make sure nothing else happens.

432
00:27:53,338 --> 00:27:54,704
If you use some common sense

433
00:27:54,873 --> 00:27:56,774
when it comes to who
stays at your house,

434
00:27:56,942 --> 00:27:59,223
you know as well as I do none
of this would have happened.

435
00:28:11,590 --> 00:28:14,992
- Dad, where are you going?
- Get breakfast.

436
00:28:17,729 --> 00:28:20,597
Ah, Elma. Elma, I
need them hotcakes.

437
00:28:20,766 --> 00:28:22,029
All right?

438
00:28:22,434 --> 00:28:25,097
Come on. BOY: Sorry, Pop.

439
00:28:34,379 --> 00:28:38,009
That's a nice-looking
cap you got there, Mitchell.

440
00:28:38,350 --> 00:28:40,342
Bill get turned up
that way by accident?

441
00:28:40,519 --> 00:28:42,715
Or do you wanna go
around looking like a moron?

442
00:28:44,456 --> 00:28:46,482
You say something
to me, old man?

443
00:28:47,226 --> 00:28:50,719
Bill up like that, you can't
keep the sun out of your eyes.

444
00:28:50,896 --> 00:28:52,558
You don't need it to
keep your head warm.

445
00:28:52,731 --> 00:28:54,131
You're too good
at lighting fires.

446
00:28:56,068 --> 00:28:59,835
Certain people need to learn certain
things just ain't done around here.

447
00:29:03,041 --> 00:29:05,738
It just seems to me

448
00:29:06,478 --> 00:29:08,913
that lighting fires

449
00:29:10,148 --> 00:29:12,549
in other people's yards

450
00:29:12,718 --> 00:29:15,085
is a pretty dumb
way to make a point.

451
00:29:15,254 --> 00:29:20,659
But if you ever get the urge to
do it again, burn something else.

452
00:29:20,826 --> 00:29:24,319
I see you and yours
in church all the time.

453
00:29:24,496 --> 00:29:26,795
I guess the cross don't
mean nothing to you.

454
00:29:27,366 --> 00:29:32,862
It just don't seem Christian to
use the cross as a symbol of hate.

455
00:29:33,038 --> 00:29:37,772
Maybe Reverend Weber
ought to talk on that sometime.

456
00:29:37,943 --> 00:29:43,541
Maybe he ought to talk on how
we're supposed to love our brothers,

457
00:29:43,949 --> 00:29:45,918
treat them like we
want them to treat us.

458
00:29:48,920 --> 00:29:50,548
Why don't you shut up?

459
00:30:02,634 --> 00:30:03,994
Two orders of eggs over easy.

460
00:30:04,136 --> 00:30:06,002
Side of bacon, side
of sausage and grits.

461
00:30:06,171 --> 00:30:07,651
Don't forget them
hotcakes. All right?

462
00:30:07,806 --> 00:30:09,172
See you got your wife working.

463
00:30:09,341 --> 00:30:10,934
And my boy. And
I'm still short-handed.

464
00:30:11,109 --> 00:30:13,874
Why don't you hire Cyrus back?

465
00:30:14,413 --> 00:30:16,507
I can't have no colored
murderer working here.

466
00:30:16,682 --> 00:30:18,742
Cyrus ain't no murderer.

467
00:30:18,917 --> 00:30:21,819
I know he ain't. But that's
what everybody thinks.

468
00:30:21,987 --> 00:30:24,115
If people stop coming in
here, I go out of business.

469
00:30:24,589 --> 00:30:26,581
How long you think you
gonna stay in business

470
00:30:26,758 --> 00:30:29,250
if people have to wait a
half hour for their grits?

471
00:30:30,829 --> 00:30:32,559
Cyrus is the best man
ever worked for me.

472
00:30:32,731 --> 00:30:35,599
If it was just me, I'd hire
him back in a second.

473
00:30:36,268 --> 00:30:38,203
But I got Elma to think
about and the young'uns.

474
00:30:39,271 --> 00:30:42,002
Did it ever occur to you
if you hired him back,

475
00:30:42,174 --> 00:30:44,109
people would see
how much you trust him.

476
00:30:44,276 --> 00:30:47,440
They might get to thinking
maybe he's not a killer.

477
00:30:47,612 --> 00:30:49,547
Pete, you got them hotcakes yet?

478
00:30:49,715 --> 00:30:52,810
Dang it, Elma, where are
them hotcakes? Come on.

479
00:31:05,764 --> 00:31:07,460
What are you doing?

480
00:31:07,833 --> 00:31:10,302
Well, I'm going over to Moley's.

481
00:31:12,237 --> 00:31:15,765
Cyrus, you can't go over
there. You won't be safe.

482
00:31:16,742 --> 00:31:20,110
Well, Mr. Matlock, you've
done enough for me already.

483
00:31:21,113 --> 00:31:23,173
Just what I'd do for
any friend of mine.

484
00:31:26,251 --> 00:31:30,279
Mr. Matlock, we ain't friends.

485
00:31:30,622 --> 00:31:32,591
And never gonna be.

486
00:31:33,692 --> 00:31:35,718
Truth of the matter is,
all this hadn't happened,

487
00:31:35,894 --> 00:31:37,795
I never would've set
one foot in your place.

488
00:31:38,797 --> 00:31:41,961
Never eat here, never
even had my family here.

489
00:31:44,136 --> 00:31:45,661
No.

490
00:31:46,405 --> 00:31:48,738
I'm best off with my own kind.

491
00:31:54,646 --> 00:31:56,274
Cyrus,

492
00:31:57,382 --> 00:32:02,184
you know a lot of them
people down there at the diner,

493
00:32:03,288 --> 00:32:04,449
ignoramuses.

494
00:32:06,558 --> 00:32:09,118
I'm gonna tell you
something I believe.

495
00:32:10,195 --> 00:32:15,566
I ain't gonna tell you that I've
liked every colored man I ever met,

496
00:32:15,734 --> 00:32:20,263
and I sure as hell ain't liked
every white man I ever met.

497
00:32:21,072 --> 00:32:23,041
But I like you.

498
00:32:23,975 --> 00:32:25,910
You've got a nice smile.

499
00:32:26,077 --> 00:32:27,511
Smart.

500
00:32:27,679 --> 00:32:29,807
Head on your shoulders.

501
00:32:30,081 --> 00:32:33,779
Make the best blueberry
pie on the face of this Earth.

502
00:32:34,019 --> 00:32:37,683
And I ain't ignoring the
fact that you're colored.

503
00:32:37,856 --> 00:32:40,325
That's part of who you are.

504
00:32:41,026 --> 00:32:43,723
But if neither the
whites nor the coloreds

505
00:32:43,895 --> 00:32:47,093
start taking it one
person at a time,

506
00:32:47,265 --> 00:32:48,756
instead of lumping
everybody together,

507
00:32:48,934 --> 00:32:52,302
then all the marching and
protesting and integrating in the world

508
00:32:52,471 --> 00:32:54,440
ain't gonna amount
to a hill of beans.

509
00:32:56,675 --> 00:32:58,200
Well...

510
00:32:59,611 --> 00:33:03,981
It really ain't that hard to
make a decent blueberry pie.

511
00:33:04,749 --> 00:33:06,877
I could probably teach you how.

512
00:33:07,886 --> 00:33:10,048
Probably not.

513
00:33:12,757 --> 00:33:14,658
Give it a try.

514
00:33:23,134 --> 00:33:26,935
Hi, Dad, how come you're
not at the service station?

515
00:33:27,105 --> 00:33:31,304
We stayed home so Cyrus could
teach me to make blueberry pie.

516
00:33:31,610 --> 00:33:33,374
You made that?

517
00:33:34,179 --> 00:33:35,374
I helped.

518
00:33:35,547 --> 00:33:37,379
What did you do?

519
00:33:38,250 --> 00:33:43,211
I picked the berries, washed
them, tried to make the crust.

520
00:33:43,388 --> 00:33:46,449
But after I'd pretty much
destroyed two batches of dough

521
00:33:46,625 --> 00:33:49,094
trying to roll it out,
Cyrus took over.

522
00:33:49,261 --> 00:33:50,661
Where you been?

523
00:33:50,829 --> 00:33:52,923
Talking to Owen Lashley.

524
00:33:53,098 --> 00:33:55,090
Last person to see
Sheriff Slattery alive.

525
00:33:55,267 --> 00:33:56,462
What did he say?

526
00:33:56,635 --> 00:33:59,366
Well, he went down
to the sheriff's station,

527
00:33:59,538 --> 00:34:03,066
unh, around 8:00 that night

528
00:34:03,241 --> 00:34:07,235
to answer questions about
that burglary at his pawnshop,

529
00:34:07,412 --> 00:34:09,938
and the sheriff sent
Sully home around 8:30

530
00:34:10,115 --> 00:34:11,947
and Owen left about
a half hour later.

531
00:34:15,053 --> 00:34:17,420
What time did Wes'
wife find him dead?

532
00:34:17,589 --> 00:34:19,524
9:15.

533
00:34:20,992 --> 00:34:25,555
So whoever killed him must
have been hiding outside the office

534
00:34:25,730 --> 00:34:27,858
waiting for Wes
to be by himself.

535
00:34:28,033 --> 00:34:29,592
Right.

536
00:34:32,170 --> 00:34:36,005
Did you say you put new radios
in all three sheriff's cars last week?

537
00:34:36,174 --> 00:34:37,198
Yup.

538
00:34:37,375 --> 00:34:42,336
Well, apparently, Sully left
his wallet at the office that night,

539
00:34:42,514 --> 00:34:46,747
and when the sheriff
radioed him to tell him,

540
00:34:46,918 --> 00:34:49,717
Owen says the static
was so bad on Sully's end,

541
00:34:49,888 --> 00:34:51,448
he could hardly hear
what he was saying.

542
00:34:51,590 --> 00:34:52,990
Static?

543
00:34:53,158 --> 00:34:55,218
That radio was
fresh from the factory.

544
00:34:56,494 --> 00:34:58,292
Well, thought you'd wanna know.

545
00:35:10,942 --> 00:35:11,966
Been nearly a half hour.

546
00:35:12,143 --> 00:35:13,304
When's your daddy gonna call?

547
00:35:13,478 --> 00:35:16,004
Soon as he gets to
where Sully was that night.

548
00:35:16,381 --> 00:35:19,146
I really appreciate
your doing this, Owen.

549
00:35:19,317 --> 00:35:21,752
Things like this
drive my father crazy.

550
00:35:21,920 --> 00:35:26,051
He's got to know whether it's the radio
or the work he did installing it is bad.

551
00:35:26,224 --> 00:35:29,285
<i>Oh, Ben, it's Charlie.</i>

552
00:35:29,461 --> 00:35:31,487
<i>You there? Hello?</i>

553
00:35:31,863 --> 00:35:33,354
I'm here, Dad. Where are you?

554
00:35:33,531 --> 00:35:37,093
Chesterfield Road, about
two blocks from Sully's house.

555
00:35:37,268 --> 00:35:38,292
How's the static?

556
00:35:39,638 --> 00:35:41,106
Nothing like it was that night.

557
00:35:41,272 --> 00:35:43,366
Dad, you're coming
in clear as a bell.

558
00:35:44,609 --> 00:35:46,805
That don't make no sense.

559
00:35:46,978 --> 00:35:48,913
Maybe Sully said he
was somewheres else

560
00:35:49,080 --> 00:35:50,343
and Owen heard it wrong.

561
00:35:51,182 --> 00:35:54,277
Before he left the office, he
said he's going home for supper.

562
00:35:54,452 --> 00:35:57,581
Owen said Sully said he
was going straight home, Dad.

563
00:35:58,590 --> 00:36:01,321
<i>So can Owen go now?</i>

564
00:36:02,227 --> 00:36:05,391
No. Don't let Owen
leave until I call you back.

565
00:36:05,563 --> 00:36:07,623
You hear me?
Don't let him leave.

566
00:36:10,802 --> 00:36:11,842
I gotta go. You know what?

567
00:36:12,003 --> 00:36:14,632
I'm as crazy as your
pa for staying this long.

568
00:36:14,806 --> 00:36:17,207
<i>Hello, Ben, is Owen still there?</i>

569
00:36:18,743 --> 00:36:22,271
That's it. That's the
way it sounded that night.

570
00:36:22,447 --> 00:36:25,144
We could hardly hear you for
the static, Dad. Where are you?

571
00:36:30,221 --> 00:36:32,747
A lot closer than before.

572
00:36:39,164 --> 00:36:40,757
Sully.

573
00:36:48,807 --> 00:36:50,969
- Hello, Sully.
- Sully.

574
00:36:51,142 --> 00:36:53,976
Hello, Ben, Charlie.
What brings you out here?

575
00:36:54,145 --> 00:36:56,046
We need to talk to you, Sully.

576
00:36:56,214 --> 00:36:58,274
Yeah? About what?

577
00:36:58,450 --> 00:37:02,945
About, uh, the burglary over
at Owen Lashley's pawnshop.

578
00:37:03,121 --> 00:37:04,783
How's the investigation going?

579
00:37:04,956 --> 00:37:07,755
It's coming. Slow but sure.

580
00:37:07,926 --> 00:37:10,794
Found that 50-dollar bill with
the phone number on it yet?

581
00:37:12,263 --> 00:37:15,131
Who told you about that? - Owen.

582
00:37:15,300 --> 00:37:18,862
As soon as we found out you
were behind the sheriff's office, uh,

583
00:37:19,037 --> 00:37:22,804
when Wes radioed you about
losing your wallet that night,

584
00:37:22,974 --> 00:37:25,102
we asked Owen a
whole lot of questions.

585
00:37:25,376 --> 00:37:26,896
I don't know what
you're talking about.

586
00:37:27,512 --> 00:37:29,913
We just come from
the bank, Sully.

587
00:37:30,081 --> 00:37:33,245
Vera. You know,
teller. Tall. Red hair.

588
00:37:33,418 --> 00:37:37,583
She remembers that big deposit
you made the day after the robbery.

589
00:37:37,756 --> 00:37:39,748
One of the bills had
a phone number on it.

590
00:37:39,924 --> 00:37:43,622
She asked if you needed
to copy it down, you said no.

591
00:37:44,062 --> 00:37:46,429
That's why you killed
the sheriff, isn't it?

592
00:37:46,598 --> 00:37:48,692
So he wouldn't find
out you were the burglar.

593
00:37:50,468 --> 00:37:53,802
Ooh, you boys are just
barking up the wrong tree.

594
00:37:56,641 --> 00:38:00,339
We told Deputy Vance
and he told the state patrol.

595
00:38:00,512 --> 00:38:02,913
Come clean and they
might go easy on you, Sully.

596
00:38:04,582 --> 00:38:06,813
Burglary's one thing,

597
00:38:07,218 --> 00:38:11,519
but this don't prove I'm a murderer,
especially seeing that I'm white.

598
00:38:11,856 --> 00:38:14,018
Your boy's colored.

599
00:38:14,192 --> 00:38:17,219
Compared to that,
this don't mean nothing.

600
00:38:40,885 --> 00:38:43,013
How come you're here so late?

601
00:38:43,188 --> 00:38:44,520
Couldn't sleep.

602
00:38:44,689 --> 00:38:47,318
Oh, sit down. I'll cut
you a piece of pie.

603
00:38:47,692 --> 00:38:49,752
No thanks, Pop.

604
00:38:51,696 --> 00:38:55,030
How about a couple
of hands of gin?

605
00:38:56,801 --> 00:38:59,464
Hmm, I don't know if
that's such a good idea.

606
00:38:59,637 --> 00:39:02,630
You know how you
get when you lose.

607
00:39:03,441 --> 00:39:05,740
Just that once.

608
00:39:05,910 --> 00:39:07,830
That glass of tomato
juice slipped off the table.

609
00:39:07,979 --> 00:39:10,278
I didn't do it on purpose.

610
00:39:10,782 --> 00:39:13,411
Soaked clear
through my overalls.

611
00:39:13,585 --> 00:39:15,986
It was an accident, Dad.

612
00:39:29,300 --> 00:39:32,702
Are all the face cards
on top of this deck?

613
00:39:33,504 --> 00:39:35,803
You shuffled. I didn't.

614
00:39:43,948 --> 00:39:45,576
You sure this is a full deck?

615
00:39:45,750 --> 00:39:47,548
See? You getting testy already.

616
00:39:47,919 --> 00:39:49,353
No, I'm not.

617
00:39:51,823 --> 00:39:53,223
- Gin.
- Gin?

618
00:40:00,198 --> 00:40:02,190
I'm sorry, Dad.

619
00:40:02,367 --> 00:40:07,704
Uh, at least the tomato
juice washed out.

620
00:40:08,139 --> 00:40:11,234
Don't move. Just sit
there. Uh, I'll clean it up.

621
00:40:12,310 --> 00:40:16,509
Your mother always said
blueberry stains won't come out.

622
00:40:17,482 --> 00:40:19,849
Oh, Lord, it's not coming out.

623
00:40:27,292 --> 00:40:28,658
Ben.

624
00:40:29,627 --> 00:40:31,152
Son.

625
00:40:38,536 --> 00:40:41,529
I understand you were recently
charged with a crime, deputy.

626
00:40:41,706 --> 00:40:44,232
Objection. That is irrelevant
to these proceedings.

627
00:40:44,409 --> 00:40:46,002
Goes to motive, Your Honor.

628
00:40:46,177 --> 00:40:48,078
Motive? But who's on trial here?

629
00:40:48,246 --> 00:40:49,441
The wrong man,

630
00:40:49,614 --> 00:40:52,083
and if you'll let me
continue, I'll prove it.

631
00:40:53,084 --> 00:40:54,712
Go ahead.

632
00:40:56,187 --> 00:40:58,986
What crime were you
charged with, Deputy Hayes?

633
00:41:00,091 --> 00:41:03,323
They say I stole some money
from Owen Lashley's pawnshop.

634
00:41:03,661 --> 00:41:05,323
Prior to the sheriff's death,

635
00:41:05,496 --> 00:41:09,365
who in the department
normally investigated burglaries?

636
00:41:10,101 --> 00:41:11,467
Me.

637
00:41:11,636 --> 00:41:13,696
But when you and the
sheriff interviewed Owen

638
00:41:13,871 --> 00:41:16,272
about the burglary at his place,

639
00:41:16,441 --> 00:41:18,410
a crime with which
you've been charged,

640
00:41:18,576 --> 00:41:20,704
the sheriff said he'd
investigate the case, didn't he?

641
00:41:21,312 --> 00:41:22,746
Yeah.

642
00:41:22,981 --> 00:41:24,882
Must have come as quite a shock.

643
00:41:25,049 --> 00:41:28,952
After all, you were counting on
investigating that case, weren't you,

644
00:41:29,120 --> 00:41:31,180
so you could make
sure it was never solved?

645
00:41:31,356 --> 00:41:33,587
- That ain't true.
- You mean, the fact

646
00:41:33,758 --> 00:41:35,989
that the sheriff wound up dead

647
00:41:36,160 --> 00:41:39,597
before he could look into that
burglary is just a big coincidence?

648
00:41:39,764 --> 00:41:41,289
That's right.

649
00:41:41,466 --> 00:41:44,698
Everybody knows that that
colored fellow over there done it.

650
00:41:44,869 --> 00:41:47,600
He got mad at Wes
for insulting his cooking

651
00:41:47,772 --> 00:41:50,241
and he killed
him with his knife.

652
00:41:51,642 --> 00:41:54,874
His kind do stuff
like that all the time.

653
00:41:55,380 --> 00:41:58,873
And all you're doing
is muddying the water,

654
00:41:59,050 --> 00:42:01,645
trying to confuse them
good people over there.

655
00:42:06,924 --> 00:42:11,794
Before your arrest, you had a key
to just about every place of business

656
00:42:11,963 --> 00:42:13,693
in Mount Harlan, didn't you?

657
00:42:13,865 --> 00:42:15,857
For when you pulled
night patrol duty.

658
00:42:16,034 --> 00:42:17,093
Yeah. That's right.

659
00:42:17,268 --> 00:42:19,362
- Have a key to the pawnshop?
- Yeah.

660
00:42:20,038 --> 00:42:22,200
You also had a key
to the diner, didn't you?

661
00:42:24,042 --> 00:42:25,066
Yeah.

662
00:42:25,243 --> 00:42:27,508
You used that key
to get into the diner

663
00:42:27,678 --> 00:42:30,045
after you left the sheriff's
office that night, didn't you?

664
00:42:30,214 --> 00:42:31,238
Most certainly did not.

665
00:42:31,416 --> 00:42:35,012
And being real careful not to
disturb any of the fingerprints

666
00:42:35,186 --> 00:42:37,348
or blueberry juice
that was on it,

667
00:42:37,522 --> 00:42:39,753
you stole one of
Cyrus Jordan's knives.

668
00:42:39,924 --> 00:42:41,449
Didn't you? SULLY: No.

669
00:42:41,626 --> 00:42:43,595
And then you went
to your patrol car,

670
00:42:44,028 --> 00:42:46,190
and instead of going home,

671
00:42:46,364 --> 00:42:51,496
you waited in your car outside the
back of the sheriff's station. Didn't you?

672
00:42:51,669 --> 00:42:53,399
No. That ain't true.

673
00:42:53,571 --> 00:42:55,171
You knew the sheriff
would be calling you

674
00:42:55,339 --> 00:42:59,174
because you accidentally on
purpose left your wallet there.

675
00:42:59,343 --> 00:43:00,423
What are you talking about?

676
00:43:00,578 --> 00:43:02,774
So you waited and waited,

677
00:43:03,347 --> 00:43:06,545
and sure enough, he called you,

678
00:43:06,717 --> 00:43:09,915
giving you a chance to
make him and Owen think

679
00:43:10,088 --> 00:43:13,024
that you were just
two blocks from home,

680
00:43:13,191 --> 00:43:16,286
so later on, Owen
could give you an alibi.

681
00:43:16,461 --> 00:43:17,520
Isn't that right, Sully?

682
00:43:17,695 --> 00:43:18,815
No. That ain't right.

683
00:43:18,963 --> 00:43:22,866
And after that, you
waited till Owen was gone,

684
00:43:23,301 --> 00:43:25,668
and then you snuck
in the sheriff's office

685
00:43:25,837 --> 00:43:27,362
through the back door,

686
00:43:27,538 --> 00:43:31,532
and then used the knife from
the diner to kill Sheriff Slattery

687
00:43:31,709 --> 00:43:35,908
so no one would ever find
out you were a common thief.

688
00:43:36,080 --> 00:43:38,709
- Didn't you?
- No.

689
00:43:39,050 --> 00:43:40,882
Yes, you did, Sully.

690
00:43:41,052 --> 00:43:43,988
I know because I got proof.

691
00:43:44,155 --> 00:43:46,317
I got proof right here.

692
00:43:46,491 --> 00:43:50,451
Deputy Vance removed
this from your patrol car,

693
00:43:50,628 --> 00:43:52,597
the one you were
driving that night.

694
00:43:53,064 --> 00:43:56,057
It's the floor mat
from the driver's side.

695
00:43:58,603 --> 00:44:02,096
Mind telling the court what
made these stains, Sully?

696
00:44:03,641 --> 00:44:06,907
They're blueberry
stains, aren't they, Sully?

697
00:44:09,213 --> 00:44:10,647
No, uh...

698
00:44:12,183 --> 00:44:14,778
They're... It's grape juice.

699
00:44:14,952 --> 00:44:17,183
I was eating in my
car and it spilled.

700
00:44:17,622 --> 00:44:19,056
Well...

701
00:44:19,423 --> 00:44:23,383
if that were the case, those stains
would be on top of the floor mat,

702
00:44:23,561 --> 00:44:26,360
not on the bottom. No.

703
00:44:26,631 --> 00:44:30,534
The police lab over in Atlanta
says these are blueberry stains.

704
00:44:30,701 --> 00:44:31,896
How did they get there?

705
00:44:32,069 --> 00:44:33,093
Hmm. MATLOCK: Well,

706
00:44:33,271 --> 00:44:36,605
after you stole Cyrus' knife
from the diner that night,

707
00:44:36,874 --> 00:44:38,672
you had to keep it out of sight

708
00:44:38,843 --> 00:44:42,143
while you were waiting for
Owen to leave the sheriff's office.

709
00:44:42,313 --> 00:44:47,877
So you put it down by your
feet underneath the floor mat.

710
00:44:48,319 --> 00:44:50,447
Didn't you, Sully?

711
00:45:08,606 --> 00:45:11,770
Next morning, the jury
found Cyrus innocent,

712
00:45:11,943 --> 00:45:15,903
and by the end of the day, he
was back working at the diner.

713
00:45:16,080 --> 00:45:19,209
And me and the children
came back the day after.

714
00:45:19,383 --> 00:45:23,320
And from then on, life
seemed to get better.

715
00:45:23,487 --> 00:45:27,219
Cyrus seemed more at
peace with hisself somehow,

716
00:45:27,391 --> 00:45:33,422
and Gamble, well, heh, white folks
finding his daddy innocent like that

717
00:45:33,598 --> 00:45:38,127
gave him something he'd been
missing his whole life: hope.

718
00:45:39,804 --> 00:45:40,965
Where's Gamble now?

719
00:45:41,138 --> 00:45:44,575
Columbus, Ohio. Got his
own advertising company.

720
00:45:44,742 --> 00:45:45,766
Oh.

721
00:45:45,943 --> 00:45:48,435
And Cora, she's
married, mother of 3.

722
00:45:48,613 --> 00:45:49,637
Three?

723
00:45:51,048 --> 00:45:53,347
And what about Cyrus?

724
00:45:55,219 --> 00:45:58,314
That's, heh, why I
called your daddy.

725
00:45:58,489 --> 00:46:02,085
Cyrus passed away
three weeks ago.

726
00:46:02,260 --> 00:46:03,284
I'm so sorry.

727
00:46:04,662 --> 00:46:07,564
He was a fine man,
Martha. I'll never forget him.

728
00:46:09,467 --> 00:46:11,347
I got something I wanna show you.
- Hmm?

729
00:46:11,502 --> 00:46:13,095
Close your eyes.

730
00:46:18,342 --> 00:46:22,905
Okay. Now open them.

731
00:46:23,080 --> 00:46:25,743
Aah! You've still
got that old car.

732
00:46:25,916 --> 00:46:28,112
Oh, it was my
father's pride and joy.

733
00:46:28,286 --> 00:46:30,778
He was a good man too.

734
00:46:30,955 --> 00:46:36,053
Your daddy and granddaddy
set our lives on a good course.

735
00:46:36,227 --> 00:46:42,929
I just thought somebody ought
to thank him before it's too late.

